# --- test scratch dirs INSIDE the source tree: RETIRED 2026-09-21 ---
# `.gitignore` is not a fix — it hides dirt, it does not stop the writer, and it
# only covers the spellings someone remembered to list (the measured leak was
# 5 `internal/tools/firstopen-device-*/` directories holding 341 untracked test
# `.db` files, plus 106 of them captured in a commit, c2f5a9de). The two
# producers are GONE and must not come back:
#   internal/tools/firstopen_sync_test.go        -> testutil.TestDeviceScratchDir(t, "firstopen")
#   internal/testutil/ledgerpin_backing_test.go  -> testutil.TestDeviceScratchDir(t, "ledgerpin")
# TestDeviceScratchDir (internal/testutil/scratchroot.go) places a device fixture
# on the shared block-backed scratch home — off `/` (Rule 22) and OUTSIDE every
# checkout — and MEASURES the backing, skipping where there is no device. That is
# the property the in-tree directories were reaching for, obtained without writing
# into a source tree where a `git add` run can capture it mid-flight (the
# repo-root junk gate checks repo-ROOT paths only and is blind to a nested tree).
# The gate internal/standards/db_scaffold_gate_test.go fails any test that
# recreates a scratch DIRECTORY inside internal/ (arm 2). These patterns stay as
# the belt-and-braces layer for residue created before the fix:
firstopen-device-*/
ledgerpin-device-*/

# --- repo-root report docs — the parking-lot class ---
# See devdoc/repo-workspace-hygiene-2026-09-12.md: 37 point-in-time report .md
# files accumulated at the repo root between 2026-08-22 and 2026-09-12 and were
# committed ONE BY ONE; they now live under reports/archive/<YYYY-MM>/ (a report)
# or devdoc/ (a design narrative). Ignoring root markdown stops a new one from
# showing up as a `??` in `git status` that a single `git add .` sweeps in. The
# negations are the deliberate exceptions, not oversights: CHANGELOG.md is
# the release surface, and CONFIG.md and COMMANDS.md are the two DOCUMENTS OF
# RECORD (same anchor discipline, each with its own gate in internal/standards):
# a knob question sends the reader to CONFIG.md and a command question to
# COMMANDS.md, so both belong where the reader looks.
#
# PROJECT.md IS the fourth negation (2026-10-19): the split of the root rule
# file by CONTENT KIND. Rules 1-41 are the project's engineering contract — they
# hold in any clone on any machine (internal Go standards, internal/standards
# gates, devdoc references, `make test-budget`) — so they are TRACKED, as
# PROJECT.md, beside CONFIG.md and COMMANDS.md. Everything host-local (this
# machine's addresses, ssh aliases, task-sync pins, the operator's chat-policy
# fence) stayed behind in GOPI.md.
#
# GOPI.md carries NO negation, deliberately (2026-10-04): it is the CLIENT's own
# rule file — gopi still loads it from the run's cwd (internal/agent's
# project-context walk), but git must not carry it from machine to machine. It is
# ignored here like any other root doc, so it appears in NEITHER half of
# `git status` and nobody has to remember a `git rm --cached` step. A checkout
# gets the file from its operator, not from a clone. Do NOT restore the
# `!/GOPI.md` whitelist: that whitelist is the mechanism that propagated one
# machine's local policy into every other machine's checkout — the split above
# exists so that the TRACKED half can travel without it.
#
# Guarded by
# internal/standards TestRepoRootHasNoTrackedJunk (+ its red-proof), so a
# reviewed root doc is added to rootDocAllowlist — never by deleting this rule.
/*.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/CONFIG.md
!/COMMANDS.md
!/INSTALL.md
!/PROJECT.md
!/README.md
.heavy-skipped.tsv

# --- docs/archive/demo: run output of a tracked generator (see devdoc/demo-artifacts-untracked.md) ---
# Guarded by internal/standards TestDemoArtifactsAreUnderTheSizeCeiling.
#
# Each pattern names ONE class that a tracked generator RE-PRODUCES into the
# tree: `scripts/materials/*.sh` renders materials/<entry>/03-raw.png (the
# command's terminal capture) and 04-annotated.png (the same frame with the
# annotation boxes painted on) from that entry's own 01-input.txt/02-output.txt;
# `scripts/human-ui/build.sh` records a live chat session into
# human/{raw,clean,frames}/ plus the delivered hero/chat stills and the film;
# `docs/archive/demo/final/build.sh --shot` renders the page it generates to
# render-proof.png. The recipe and its text input stay tracked — the RENDER does
# not, because a clone should not pay 109 MB for pictures it can rebuild.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED, every one: a bare `03-raw.png` would also ignore any future
# same-named file at any depth (GOPI.md Rule 13's mirror discipline — the pattern
# exists once, where the class is defined).
#
# SIBLING ROWS own `docs/archive/demo/replay/`: task 01a0fd20 (clear that directory's
# remaining run artefacts) and 01a0fd14 (move the replay verb's output to the
# session directory altogether). This block therefore names NO replay class —
# the patterns below are master's own, added by the row that untracked one
# replay arm (`/docs/archive/demo/replay/arm-*/` + six bare-product names), and their
# block carries its own reasoning. Nothing here hides that row's remaining work.
/docs/archive/demo/materials/*/03-raw.png
/docs/archive/demo/materials/*/04-annotated.png
# The memory-loop figures are the same two shapes, rendered into docs/archive/demo/loop/
# by `bash scripts/demo-memory-loop-figs.sh` (its OUT argument) from the loop's
# own 00-entry.md/02-output.txt — the script's header states the shape is
# "docs/archive/demo/materials 的约定".
/docs/archive/demo/loop/*/03-raw.png
/docs/archive/demo/loop/*/04-annotated.png
/docs/archive/demo/human/frames/
/docs/archive/demo/human/raw/
/docs/archive/demo/human/clean/
/docs/archive/demo/human/chat-loop.gif
/docs/archive/demo/human/hero.png
/docs/archive/demo/human/chat.png
/docs/archive/demo/final/render-proof.png

# --- replay RUN artefacts — a session's own evidence, never repo content ---
# A replay arm is the recording of ONE session, written where the run happens:
# the launcher names the directory after the session it replays
# (`<OUT>/arm-<8 chars of the session id>-<YYYYMMDD>`, and `<OUT>` itself when
# no --out-tag is given). One such directory — `arm-sess-4ca8cd29-20261002`,
# 12 files / 12 MB — was committed to this tree, and it is the class this
# block exists to keep out: replay.json, the frames/, the GIF and the redaction
# artefacts are the ARM's evidence, regenerated by re-running the session, so
# they belong in the run's own session directory (~/.gopi/sessions/<task-id>/,
# outside every checkout) or a sandbox (Rule 34) — never in a tracked path.
#
# The pattern is deliberately the NARROWEST spelling that covers that class:
# it is rooted at docs/archive/demo/replay/ and matches the `arm-` prefix, so
# README.md, SOURCE-MAP.tsv, the *.py / *.sh tooling, `replay-film/` (the
# curated film, which IS repo content) and the sibling evidence-*/, img-*/,
# terminal/, probe/, appendix/ trees are untouched. Measured rather than
# assumed: `git ls-files -z | git check-ignore --no-index -z --stdin` matches
# ZERO tracked paths after this block.
/docs/archive/demo/replay/arm-*/

# The same run with no --out-tag lands its products DIRECTLY in
# docs/archive/demo/replay/ instead of inside an `arm-*` directory. These six names are
# that run's own output (the names are the arm's, not the repo's) and none of
# them is repo content — anchored on the full path so `replay-film/replay.json`
# and friends, which are curated and tracked, keep their exemption.
/docs/archive/demo/replay/frames/
/docs/archive/demo/replay/replay.json
/docs/archive/demo/replay/chat-replay.gif
/docs/archive/demo/replay/diff-table.tsv
/docs/archive/demo/replay/mask-boxes.json
/docs/archive/demo/replay/redaction-span.json

